1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,961
<font color="#FFFFFF">What if an AI could not only tell you where a
street photo was taken,</font>

2
00:00:05,041 --> 00:00:06,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">but explain how it knew?</font>

3
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:08,777
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces GeoReasoner,</font>

4
00:00:08,857 --> 00:00:14,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">a large vision-language model for street-view
geo-localization that reasons like a human.</font>

5
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:20,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors tackle two long-standing obstacles:
street-view training data is full of low-quality</font>

6
00:00:21,032 --> 00:00:22,794
<font color="#FFFFFF">images with no visual clues,</font>

7
00:00:22,874 --> 00:00:27,664
<font color="#FFFFFF">and existing localization models are black boxes
that give no explanation.</font>

8
00:00:27,744 --> 00:00:32,073
<font color="#FFFFFF">GeoReasoner solves both by quantifying how
locatable each image is,</font>

9
00:00:32,153 --> 00:00:36,614
<font color="#FFFFFF">borrowing human inference knowledge from real
geo-localization games,</font>

10
00:00:36,694 --> 00:00:39,246
<font color="#FFFFFF">and fine-tuning in two dedicated stages.</font>

11
00:00:39,326 --> 00:00:45,103
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result beats comparable vision-language models
by over twenty-five percent at country</font>

12
00:00:45,183 --> 00:00:48,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">level and thirty-eight percent at city level,</font>

13
00:00:48,144 --> 00:00:52,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">while matching a specialist trained on fifteen
times more data.</font>

14
00:00:52,590 --> 00:00:56,213
<font color="#FFFFFF">Predicting where a street-view photo was taken is
useful for urban planning,</font>

15
00:00:56,293 --> 00:00:59,915
<font color="#FFFFFF">navigation, and social studies.</font>

16
00:00:59,995 --> 00:01:03,618
<font color="#FFFFFF">But today's approaches have two blind spots.</font>

17
00:01:03,698 --> 00:01:07,321
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

18
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:11,024
<font color="#FFFFFF">the data problem: street-view datasets are stuffed
with images captured in tunnels,</font>

19
00:01:11,104 --> 00:01:14,726
<font color="#FFFFFF">against blank walls, or of generic vegetation,</font>

20
00:01:14,806 --> 00:01:18,429
<font color="#FFFFFF">none of which contain clues a model could use to
locate them.</font>

21
00:01:18,509 --> 00:01:22,132
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

22
00:01:22,212 --> 00:01:25,835
<font color="#FFFFFF">the reasoning problem: retrieval and classification
models operate as black boxes,</font>

23
00:01:25,915 --> 00:01:29,537
<font color="#FFFFFF">handing back a coordinate with no explanation a
person could inspect or trust.</font>

24
00:01:29,617 --> 00:01:33,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper argues that both quality and
interpretability must be fixed together.</font>

25
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:38,342
<font color="#FFFFFF">Why now, and why this approach?</font>

26
00:01:38,422 --> 00:01:43,145
<font color="#FFFFFF">Large vision-language models have shown they can
fuse images and text and follow step-by-step reasoning,</font>

27
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:47,947
<font color="#FFFFFF">and prior research shows that adding a reasoning
process makes language models stronger.</font>

28
00:01:48,027 --> 00:01:52,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">Meanwhile,</font>

29
00:01:52,830 --> 00:01:57,552
<font color="#FFFFFF">a huge untapped resource exists: communities behind
geo-localization games have spent</font>

30
00:01:57,632 --> 00:02:02,355
<font color="#FFFFFF">years assembling textual clues that pinpoint
countries and cities from subtle visual details.</font>

31
00:02:02,435 --> 00:02:07,157
<font color="#FFFFFF">GeoReasoner's insight is to harvest that human
inference knowledge and pair it with high-quality street views,</font>

32
00:02:07,237 --> 00:02:11,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the model learns not just to guess a location
but to justify it.</font>

33
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:14,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">The work makes three contributions.</font>

34
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:20,349
<font color="#FFFFFF">It introduces a new paradigm that leverages a large
vision-language model together with</font>

35
00:02:20,429 --> 00:02:24,393
<font color="#FFFFFF">external human reasoning knowledge learned from
online games,</font>

36
00:02:24,473 --> 00:02:28,172
<font color="#FFFFFF">enabling geo-localization that comes with an explanation.</font>

37
00:02:28,252 --> 00:02:30,758
<font color="#FFFFFF">It defines the concept of locatability,</font>

38
00:02:30,838 --> 00:02:34,006
<font color="#FFFFFF">a metric for how findable an image's location is,</font>

39
00:02:34,086 --> 00:02:39,575
<font color="#FFFFFF">and builds a CLIP-based network to compute it,
which drives the curation of a clean,</font>

40
00:02:39,655 --> 00:02:41,299
<font color="#FFFFFF">high-quality training set.</font>

41
00:02:41,379 --> 00:02:43,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">And it delivers GeoReasoner itself,</font>

42
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:50,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">a model that beats existing geo-localization
systems while offering detailed reasoning for every prediction.</font>

43
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:52,883
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method has two halves.</font>

44
00:02:52,963 --> 00:02:54,339
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, data curation.</font>

45
00:02:54,419 --> 00:03:00,371
<font color="#FFFFFF">GeoReasoner introduces a locatability metric:
MaskFormer segments each street view into</font>

46
00:03:00,451 --> 00:03:02,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">semantic classes like buildings,</font>

47
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:06,265
<font color="#FFFFFF">sky, and vehicles, producing a vector of area ratios.</font>

48
00:03:06,345 --> 00:03:12,991
<font color="#FFFFFF">Sentence-BERT then measures how relevant each class
is to the textual clues mined from geo-games,</font>

49
00:03:13,071 --> 00:03:14,794
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing a weight vector.</font>

50
00:03:14,874 --> 00:03:22,352
<font color="#FFFFFF">The locatability of an image is simply the weighted
sum of its class ratios against these importance weights,</font>

51
00:03:22,432 --> 00:03:26,859
<font color="#FFFFFF">and images scoring above a threshold of zero point
four are kept.</font>

52
00:03:26,939 --> 00:03:33,793
<font color="#FFFFFF">This filters over one hundred thirty thousand raw
images down to seventy thousand high-quality ones.</font>

53
00:03:33,873 --> 00:03:35,041
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, the model.</font>

54
00:03:35,121 --> 00:03:39,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">Built on Qwen-VL with a vision encoder, a
vision-language adapter,</font>

55
00:03:39,697 --> 00:03:41,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a pre-trained language model,</font>

56
00:03:41,985 --> 00:03:48,215
<font color="#FFFFFF">GeoReasoner is fine-tuned in two stages using
lightweight LoRA adapters: a reasoning-tuning</font>

57
00:03:48,295 --> 00:03:52,445
<font color="#FFFFFF">stage that learns country-level explanations from
game clues,</font>

58
00:03:52,525 --> 00:03:59,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">followed by a location-tuning stage that sharpens
city-level accuracy on the curated street views.</font>

59
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:03,611
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper builds its data from scratch.</font>

60
00:04:03,691 --> 00:04:07,682
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using OpenStreetMap road networks and the Google
Street View API,</font>

61
00:04:07,762 --> 00:04:11,752
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors sample points every four thousand
meters across the top global cities,</font>

62
00:04:11,832 --> 00:04:15,823
<font color="#FFFFFF">collecting more than one hundred thirty thousand
geo-tagged street views spanning seventy-two</font>

63
00:04:15,903 --> 00:04:19,894
<font color="#FFFFFF">cities in forty-eight countries.</font>

64
00:04:19,974 --> 00:04:23,965
<font color="#FFFFFF">Applying the locatability filter at a threshold of
zero point four yields roughly seventy</font>

65
00:04:24,045 --> 00:04:28,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">thousand high-quality images.</font>

66
00:04:28,115 --> 00:04:32,106
<font color="#FFFFFF">Separately,</font>

67
00:04:32,186 --> 00:04:36,177
<font color="#FFFFFF">they scrape over three thousand textual clues from
two geo-localization games,</font>

68
00:04:36,257 --> 00:04:40,248
<font color="#FFFFFF">GeoGuessr and Tuxun,</font>

69
00:04:40,328 --> 00:04:44,318
<font color="#FFFFFF">cleaning them with a BERT-based entity recognizer
and pairing each with a street-view image.</font>

70
00:04:44,398 --> 00:04:48,389
<font color="#FFFFFF">For evaluation they use a held-out set of a
thousand images and,</font>

71
00:04:48,469 --> 00:04:52,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">to test generalization, the standard Im2GPS and
Im2GPS3k Flickr benchmarks.</font>

72
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:54,949
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is decisive.</font>

73
00:04:55,029 --> 00:04:56,386
<font color="#FFFFFF">Measured by F1 score,</font>

74
00:04:56,466 --> 00:05:01,448
<font color="#FFFFFF">GeoReasoner beats the strongest comparable
vision-language model, Qwen-VL,</font>

75
00:05:01,528 --> 00:05:07,947
<font color="#FFFFFF">by just over twenty-five percent at country level
and nearly thirty-nine percent at city level,</font>

76
00:05:08,027 --> 00:05:13,693
<font color="#FFFFFF">reaching a country F1 of zero point nine zero and a
city F1 of zero point eight six.</font>

77
00:05:13,773 --> 00:05:16,703
<font color="#FFFFFF">Even more striking, it edges out StreetCLIP,</font>

78
00:05:16,783 --> 00:05:23,407
<font color="#FFFFFF">a model built specifically for geo-localization and
trained on one point one million street views,</font>

79
00:05:23,487 --> 00:05:26,485
<font color="#FFFFFF">while GeoReasoner uses only seventy thousand.</font>

80
00:05:26,565 --> 00:05:32,505
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also show that as the fraction of
high-locatability images in training rises</font>

81
00:05:32,585 --> 00:05:34,763
<font color="#FFFFFF">from zero to one hundred percent,</font>

82
00:05:34,843 --> 00:05:39,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">accuracy climbs steadily, proving that the quality
of the curated data,</font>

83
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:43,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">not merely its quantity, is what powers the gains.</font>

84
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:47,857
<font color="#FFFFFF">An ablation isolates the contribution of each
fine-tuning stage.</font>

85
00:05:47,937 --> 00:05:50,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">Starting from the Qwen-VL baseline,</font>

86
00:05:50,407 --> 00:05:55,056
<font color="#FFFFFF">adding only reasoning tuning improves country and
city F1 modestly.</font>

87
00:05:55,136 --> 00:05:59,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">Adding only location tuning produces a much larger jump,</font>

88
00:05:59,088 --> 00:06:01,973
<font color="#FFFFFF">especially at the fine-grained city level,</font>

89
00:06:02,053 --> 00:06:06,772
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming that location tuning is essential for
pinpointing cities.</font>

90
00:06:06,852 --> 00:06:11,713
<font color="#FFFFFF">But the full model, with both stages stacked, is
the strongest of all,</font>

91
00:06:11,793 --> 00:06:17,641
<font color="#FFFFFF">reaching a country F1 of zero point nine zero and a
city F1 of zero point eight six.</font>

92
00:06:17,721 --> 00:06:22,511
<font color="#FFFFFF">The two stages are complementary: location tuning
supplies precision,</font>

93
00:06:22,591 --> 00:06:27,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">reasoning tuning supplies explanations and a
further accuracy lift.</font>

94
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:33,424
<font color="#FFFFFF">The numbers that matter: GeoReasoner improves on
the best comparable vision-language model</font>

95
00:06:33,504 --> 00:06:38,981
<font color="#FFFFFF">by twenty-five point zero two percent at the
country level and thirty-eight point six</font>

96
00:06:39,061 --> 00:06:41,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">one percent at the city level in F1.</font>

97
00:06:41,414 --> 00:06:47,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its full-model F1 scores are zero point nine zero
for country and zero point eight six for city.</font>

98
00:06:47,690 --> 00:06:51,533
<font color="#FFFFFF">It achieves this with just seventy thousand
training images,</font>

99
00:06:51,613 --> 00:06:57,024
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus the one point one million used by
StreetCLIP, which it nonetheless surpasses.</font>

100
00:06:57,104 --> 00:07:02,581
<font color="#FFFFFF">The underlying data comes from more than one
hundred thirty thousand raw street views</font>

101
00:07:02,661 --> 00:07:05,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">across seventy-two cities and forty-eight countries,</font>

102
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:08,203
<font color="#FFFFFF">filtered down to seventy thousand,</font>

103
00:07:08,283 --> 00:07:12,453
<font color="#FFFFFF">plus three thousand human-written reasoning clues
from geo-games.</font>

104
00:07:12,533 --> 00:07:14,675
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on the open Im2GPS benchmarks,</font>

105
00:07:14,755 --> 00:07:19,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">only ten thousand Flickr images are enough to rival
models trained on millions.</font>

106
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:22,495
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple but powerful.</font>

107
00:07:22,575 --> 00:07:28,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">You do not need millions of street-view images to
build a state-of-the-art geo-localization model.</font>

108
00:07:28,985 --> 00:07:32,176
<font color="#FFFFFF">You need the right images and the right knowledge.</font>

109
00:07:32,256 --> 00:07:37,867
<font color="#FFFFFF">By quantifying which street views are actually
locatable and by borrowing the reasoning</font>

110
00:07:37,947 --> 00:07:40,418
<font color="#FFFFFF">strategies humans use to win geo-games,</font>

111
00:07:40,498 --> 00:07:45,651
<font color="#FFFFFF">GeoReasoner matches or beats specialist models with
a fraction of the data, and,</font>

112
00:07:45,731 --> 00:07:48,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">crucially, it explains every prediction.</font>

113
00:07:48,347 --> 00:07:55,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is a case study in how data quality and human
inference knowledge can stand in for brute-force scale,</font>

114
00:07:55,150 --> 00:08:00,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">opening a path to geo-localization that is both
interpretable and resource-efficient.</font>
